chitecture, initial contact, and discovery\nL29: List-Id: \"The list is for discussion of the scope, use cases, requirements, and solutions for discovery of entities (e.g., tasks, AI agents, or endpoints) using IETF technologies.\" <dawn.ietf.org>\nL30: Archived-At: <https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/dawn/EInPLeuJXvQhWcyrj-pka2gkNws>\nL31: List-Archive: <https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/browse/dawn>\nL32: List-Help: <mailto:dawn-request@ietf.org?subject=help>\nL33: List-Owner: <mailto:dawn-owner@ietf.org>\nL34: List-Post: <mailto:dawn@ietf.org>\nL35: List-Subscribe: <mailto:dawn-join@ietf.org>\nL36: List-Unsubscribe: <mailto:dawn-leave@ietf.org>\nL37: \nL38:     Hi authors,\nL39: \nL40:     [I copied the discussions to the dmsc@ietf.org <mailto:dmsc@ietf.org> , which focuses on the capabilities based AI agent communications[https://github.com/ietf-dmsc/Charter/blob/main/dmsc-charter.md]\nL41:     Thanks for sharing the ARD architecture.( 【0†https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-bu-dawn-ard-architecture/†datatracker.ietf.org】) I have a few technical questions, particularly regarding capability classification and federated discovery.\nL42: \nL43:     1. Capability classification and interoperability\nL44: \nL45:     Section 3.1 defines capabilities as structured tokens or vocabulary terms for precise filtering, while Section 3.3.5 allows publishers to use arbitrary external namespaces.\nL46:     How can consistent capability classification and matching be achieved across different publishers and registries?\nL47: \nL48:     For example, different domains may use different terms to describe essentially the same capability. Is there an expectation of a common capability taxonomy, or will semantic matching be relied upon to resolve these differences?\nL49: \nL50:     2. Federated registry discovery and query routing\nL51: \nL52:     Section 3.2 states:\nL53:     \"A registry may crawl manifests across domains and accept registrations from publishers in other domains.\"\nL54: \nL55:     However, how does a registry know which other domains or registries to contact when searching for a particular capability?\nL56: \nL57:     For example, if Registry A receives a request for a financial analysis agent, how does it determine whether Registry B, C, or D might have relevant agents?\nL58:     DNS can locate a registry within a known domain, but it cannot identify which unknown domains may provide the requested capability.\nL59: \nL60:     Does the architecture assume preconfigured federation peers, capability advertisements, or some form of distributed registry index?\nL61: \nL62:     3. Federation scalability\nL63: \nL64:     If registries forward queries to multiple peers, how does the architecture avoid excessive query flooding, loops, and duplicated results?\nL65:     Alternatively, if registries maintain capability indices for other domains, how are these indices distributed, updated, and kept consistent as agents dynamically register or disappear?\nL66: \nL67:     I think these questions are important for understanding how the proposed architecture can scale from enterprise deployments to open, federated discovery.\nL68: \nL69:     Looking forward to the authors' thoughts.\nL70: \nL71:     Best Regards\nL72: \nL73:     Aijun\nL74: \nL75:     China Telecom\nL76:     From: dawn-bounces=40ietf.org@dmarc.ietf.org [mailto:dawn-bounces=40ietf.org@dmarc.ietf.org] On Behalf Of Junjie Bu\nL77:     Sent: Wednesday, November 4, 2026 5:22 AM\nL78:     To: dawn@ietf.org\nL79:     Subject: [Dawn] New drafts: Agentic Resource Discovery (ARD) - architecture, initial contact, and discovery\nL80: \nL81: \nL82: \nL83:     Hi,\nL84:     We have submitted a set of drafts documenting an open framework, Agentic Resource Discovery (ARD), for publishing and discovering agent capabilities (such as MCP servers, additional agents, skills, plugins, and APIs) across diverse networks:\nL85: \nL86:     - 【1†https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-bu-dawn-ard-architecture-00†datatracker.ietf.org】\nL87:     - 【2†https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-jimenez-dawn-ard-initial-contact-00†datatracker.ietf.org】\nL88:     - 【3†https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-bu-dawn-ard-discovery-00†datatracker.ietf.org】\nL89: \nL90:     The first document describes the architecture and the architectural principles this design relies on. The second document explains how initial contact is made to discover an entity through the use of DNS SVCB records, service discovery (DNS-SD), HTTP link relations, or well-known URIs. And the third document details a lookup protocol and associated data formats.\nL91:     ARD has a growing community around it and we expect it to be widely used.\nL92: \nL93: \nL94: \nL95:     We'd love to get your feedback on this set of technologies!\nL96: \nL97: \nL98: \nL99:     We also hope to be able to discuss some of this in the upcoming meeting. We believe at least parts of this, such as the initial contact, fall within the scope of the DAWN working group.\nL100: \nL101:     On behalf of the ARD authors and contributors:\nL102:     Junjie Bu, R.V. Guha, Shaun Smith, Jeffrey Damick,\nL103:     Jaime Jiménez, Mirja Kühlewind, Jari Arkko,\nL104:     Warren Kumari, Wes Hardaker, Sam Sharaf,\nL105:     + other contributors from the broader ecosystem\nL106:   * 【4†[Dawn] New drafts: Agentic Resource Discovery (AR…】  Junjie Bu\nL107:   * 【5†[Dawn] Re: New drafts: Agentic Resource Discovery…】  Aijun Wang\nL108:   * 【6†[Dawn] Re: New drafts: Agentic Resource Discovery…】  Александр Артамохов\nL109: \nL110:   * 【7†Hide Navigation Bar】\nL111: \nL112: Date\nL113: \nL114: Thread\nL115: \nL116: v2.46.0 | 【8†Report a Bug†github.com】 | By Email (tools-help@ietf.org) | 【9†System Status†status.ietf.org】\n"
